      name: "sources__fetch",
      description: 'Fetch one published source on this machine (https only, the shipped hosts plus the firm\'s approved `_hosts.json` hosts, redirects followed to five hops within them, a browser User-Agent), memoized in this server process per URL for 24 hours. An HTML page returns {kind: "html", url, final_url, status, size, sha256, blocks_total, blocks_matched?, text}: the page split into blocks (p, li, h1-h6, div, br, tr), whitespace collapsed, one block per line; `match` (a string or strings; literal, case-insensitive, any of them) keeps only the blocks containing one, and no matching block is an error. Text above 200 KB returns `truncated: true` and no text: narrow it with `match`. A PDF returns {kind: "pdf", url, status, size, sha256, saved_path}; with `save: true` the bytes are written to the overlay\'s `_fetched\\<host>\\<first 12 hex of the sha256>.pdf`, the only thing this tool writes (`saved_path` is null otherwise, and `save` on an HTML page is refused). A refused or failed fetch returns {refused: "host_not_allowed" | "fetch_failed", host, url, redirect_from?, detail?, status?, hosts_fetched}, not an error. `knowledge__write` checks excerpts against the memoized text.',
